{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Def5 gets the equality right but does not require F to be effectively axiomatized. For example, if K and F are both the set of all true arithmetic sentences, then Def5 K F holds, even though no recursively enumerable axiom set has K as its theorem set. Thus Def5 alone is too broad for Koellner’s notion of a formal system. Def6 repairs this by requiring F.RE."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Def1 supplies a type for K, not a distinguished set of absolutely provable sentences. Reading a particular K as the output of the idealized human mind is an interpretation of the parameter, not something the definition establishes."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The target fixes the language to first-order arithmetic and uses classical first-order derivability. The quoted passages speak of sentences and provability without explicitly fixing that scope."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Koellner’s F_e denotes a theorem set indexed by a natural number; Lean’s F denotes the axiom set and F.theory its theorem set. Quantifying directly over recursively enumerable axiom sets preserves WMT, since each such set has an index. The target does not represent a particular machine code or the knowledge of coincidence used in the stronger theses, neither of which WMT requires."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Def6 faithfully captures WMT within the chosen language: one effectively axiomatized system must have exactly K as its theorem set. The material mismatch is in Def5 taken on its own, where the same equality can hold for a system with no effective presentation."
}
